from a demonstrated built-in transport execution."],"assessment":"unassessed","candidate":true,"grounding":{"source_ids":["replication.go:534:665","future.go:1:314","net_transport.go:700:912"],"expectation_ids":["transport.go:1:141","future.go:1:314"],"binding_ids":["BD_pipeline_response_consumer","BD_pipeline_progress_report"],"derivation":"AppendFuture.Response explicitly requires Error to have returned and states that the response is valid only on success. This applies directly because pipelineDecode consumes AppendFuture through AppendPipeline.Consumer. The decoder calls Response without calling Error, then interprets Term and Success before updating replication progress. Consumer readiness is not documented as success. The concrete network pipeline calls future.respond(err) and delivers the future without filtering err; decodeResponse can decode a response and still return an RPC error. These observations justify checking the interface consumption relation independently of whether a harmful failed response is reachable from a built-in follower.","applicability":"Applies to the actual pipelineDecode caller of the repository's AppendFuture interface. The normative basis is the explicit method contract, not a remembered consensus rule or the observed implementation itself.","unresolved":["Whether built-in producers can supply a failed response whose fields pass the decoder's guards under a legal execution","Whether any resulting report changes commitment in an actual cluster","Whether other transport implementations impose stronger delivery guarantees"],"conflicts":[],"alternatives":["Concrete network delivery orders completion before Consumer delivery, which can establish response availability, but does not establish successful completion or satisfy the explicit Error-call requirement.","Term and Success checks may reject many failed responses, but the interface does not grant validity to response fields after failure."]},"version":1}],"bindings":[{"associations"